  cites Safari chapter Lens
  cites Safari chapter World
  cites Safari chapter RideFocal
  cites Judge chapter Grade

 RideFocal: how far the lens has pulled in this frame, which is a minimum over
 two independent distractions.

 THE DEEPER OF THE TWO WINS, and that is the whole chapter. Leaning into a corner
 narrows the lens; watching a crossing cat or gawking at roadside pigs narrows it
 too; `cam-focal` takes the smaller. So a rider doing both is no more distracted
 than the worse of them -- not the sum, which is what an implementation adding
 them would give, and which would run the focal off the bottom on a corner with
 a cat in it.

 THE LEAN FRACTION CLAMPS AT ONE. The route never banks past `max-lean`, so a
 tilt beyond it is out of range rather than a further pull-in -- and the clamp is
 graded by asking for twice the maximum and requiring the same answer.

 UNDISTRACTED IS THE BASE FOCAL EXACTLY, with no tolerance: both ramps are
 `focal * (1.0 - k * 0.0)`, and multiplying by one is exact. That is worth
 grading at 0.0 because it is the identity the whole lens rests on -- a frame
 with nothing happening must look through the design focal and not merely near
 it.

 THE CAT'S ATTENTION IS ZERO WHERE THERE IS NO CAT, and it is read from the
 segment the rider is ON rather than from the chain, so a segment without one
 contributes nothing however close the rider is to the next segment's cat.

 We say:

Section: The Chain

 Segment 0 has no cat; segment 1 does.

  segs : List Segment
  segs = [segment-at 0, segment-at 1, segment-at 2]

  rider : Integer, Real, Real, Real -> RiderState
  rider (segment) (along) (tilt) (focus) =
    RiderState { segment = segment, along = along, across = 0.0, yaw = 0.0, v = v-base,
                 tilt = tilt, heading = 0.0, gaze-yaw = 0.0, focus = focus }

  has-cat-got : List Boolean
  has-cat-got = [(list-at segs 0).has-cat, (list-at segs 1).has-cat]

  has-cat-want : List Boolean
  has-cat-want = [False, True]

Section: Nothing Happening

 No lean, no cat, no gaze: the base focal, exactly.

  idle-got : List Real
  idle-got =
    [ ride-focal segs (rider 0 0.0 0.0 0.0) - focal,
      cat-attention segs (rider 0 0.0 0.0 0.0),
      gaze-focus 0.0,
      cam-focal 0.0 0.0 - focal ]

  idle-want : List Real
  idle-want = [0.0, 0.0, 0.0, 0.0]

Section: The Lean

 More lean is a narrower lens, and past the maximum it stops narrowing.

  leaned : Real -> Real
  leaned (tilt) = ride-focal segs (rider 0 0.0 tilt 0.0)

  lean-got : List Boolean
  lean-got =
    [ leaned (max-lean / 2.0) < leaned 0.0,
      leaned max-lean < leaned (max-lean / 2.0),
      leaned (0.0 - max-lean) < leaned 0.0,
      leaned 0.0 <= focal ]

  lean-want : List Boolean
  lean-want = [True, True, True, True]

 The sign of the lean does not matter -- he is as distracted leaning either way --
 and beyond the maximum nothing more happens.

  clamp-got : List Real
  clamp-got =
    [ leaned max-lean - leaned (0.0 - max-lean),
      leaned (max-lean * 2.0) - leaned max-lean,
      leaned (max-lean * 10.0) - leaned max-lean ]

  clamp-want : List Real
  clamp-want = [0.0, 0.0, 0.0]

Section: The Distraction

 A cat the rider is closing on narrows the lens; the same rider on a segment with
 no cat is not distracted at all. Both are at the same along -- only the flag
 differs, which is the point.

 HE HAS TO BE WITHIN THE CROSSING WINDOW TO BE DISTRACTED, and at the base speed
 that window is short: `cross-t` clamps to zero beyond `cross-frames * v`, which
 is 11.4 metres past the buffer. Segment 1's cat sits at 128, so a rider at the
 segment's start is nowhere near caring about it -- graded here too, because a
 chapter that started the pull-in on sight would fail exactly that line.

  cat-got : List Boolean
  cat-got =
    [ cat-attention segs (rider 1 120.0 0.0 0.0) > 0.0,
      cat-attention segs (rider 1 0.0 0.0 0.0) > 0.0,
      cat-attention segs (rider 0 120.0 0.0 0.0) > 0.0,
      ride-focal segs (rider 1 120.0 0.0 0.0) < ride-focal segs (rider 0 120.0 0.0 0.0) ]

  cat-want : List Boolean
  cat-want = [True, False, False, True]

 A gaze narrows it too, on its own clock, and a full gaze is a deeper pull than
 no gaze.

  gaze-got : List Boolean
  gaze-got =
    [ ride-focal segs (rider 0 0.0 0.0 1.0) < ride-focal segs (rider 0 0.0 0.0 0.0),
      ride-focal segs (rider 0 0.0 0.0 1.0) < ride-focal segs (rider 0 0.0 0.0 0.5),
      gaze-focus 1.0 > gaze-focus 0.0 ]

  gaze-want : List Boolean
  gaze-want = [True, True, True]

Section: The Minimum

 THE TWO DISTRACTIONS DO NOT ADD. A rider leaning hard AND gawking answers
 exactly the deeper of the two -- so combining them changes nothing when one
 already dominates, which is what says this is a min and not a sum.

 THE COMPARISON IS `~0` AND WRITING `==` COST A RED ARM. Codex refuses a bare
 equality between Reals (CDX2085) and makes the caller say which it meant; this
 wants the bitwise one, because the claim is that the answer IS one of the two
 operands rather than close to one. The Rust interpreter ACCEPTED the `==` and
 the zig arm refused to emit at all -- which is the three-arm setup earning its
 keep, and a gap in our own front end rather than in the port.

  both : Real
  both = ride-focal segs (rider 0 0.0 max-lean 1.0)

  lean-only : Real
  lean-only = ride-focal segs (rider 0 0.0 max-lean 0.0)

  gaze-only : Real
  gaze-only = ride-focal segs (rider 0 0.0 0.0 1.0)

  min-got : List Boolean
  min-got =
    [ both <= lean-only,
      both <= gaze-only,
      both ~0 real-min lean-only gaze-only,
      lean-only + gaze-only > both ]

  min-want : List Boolean
  min-want = [True, True, True, True]

 And the same statement as arithmetic, at 0.0: the answer IS one of the two, not
 something between them.

  is-one-got : List Real
  is-one-got = [both - real-min lean-only gaze-only, cam-focal 1.0 0.0 - lean-only]

  is-one-want : List Real
  is-one-want = [0.0, 0.0]

Section: Entry

  opening : [Console] Nothing = act
    print-line-uni (grade-bools "rf-hascat" has-cat-got has-cat-want)
    print-line-uni (grade-reals "rf-idle  " idle-got idle-want 0.0)
    print-line-uni (grade-bools "rf-lean  " lean-got lean-want)
    print-line-uni (grade-reals "rf-clamp " clamp-got clamp-want 0.0)
    print-line-uni (grade-bools "rf-cat   " cat-got cat-want)
    print-line-uni (grade-bools "rf-gaze  " gaze-got gaze-want)
    print-line-uni (grade-bools "rf-min   " min-got min-want)
    print-line-uni (grade-reals "rf-isone " is-one-got is-one-want 0.0)
  end
